   "## The daughter of a king",
   "Nest was born in about 1085, the daughter of Rhys ap Tewdwr, king of Deheubarth, and his wife Gwladus. Her father ruled the whole of the south-west, from the Dyfi to the Tywi and the coasts of Dyfed, from his royal seat at Dinefwr. He was the last great king of the old line of Hywel Dda in the south, and in 1081 he had stood beside Gruffudd ap Cynan at the battle of Mynydd Carn.",
   "In 1093 the world of her childhood ended. Rhys ap Tewdwr was killed fighting the Normans near Brecon, and the chronicle of the princes wrote that with him fell the kingdom of the Britons. The Norman lords poured into the south. Nest’s brother Gruffudd, still a small boy, was carried to safety in Ireland. Nest herself was taken into the hands of the conquerors and brought up at the English court.",
   "## At the king’s court",
   "At the court of Henry I the young princess of Deheubarth was a prize and a hostage, the daughter of a fallen king whose name still meant much to the Welsh of the south. She bore the king a son, Henry, who grew up a royal lord with lands in Wales. Then Henry I gave her in marriage to one of his trusted men, Gerald of Windsor, the steward of Pembroke Castle.",
   "Gerald was the man who had held Pembroke through the great Welsh rising of 1094 to 1096, when every other castle in the south-west fell. Through Nest, the daughter of Rhys, his family gained a claim on the loyalty of the land. Through Gerald, Nest kept a place in her own country. Together they had at least four children: William, Maurice, David, and a daughter, Angharad. The marriage of the Welsh princess and the Norman lord would shape the history of three nations.",
   "## Cenarth Bychan, Christmas 1109",
   "In 1108 Gerald built a new castle at a place called Cenarth Bychan, which many have placed at Cilgerran above the Teifi gorge, and there he brought Nest and their children. At Christmas 1109 Cadwgan ap Bleddyn, king of Powys, held a great feast for the nobles of Wales at his court in Ceredigion. His son Owain heard there of the beauty of Nest, his own kinswoman, and went to see her.",
   "The chronicle tells what followed. One night Owain came back with a small band of men, broke into the castle and set it on fire. Gerald woke to the shouting and the smoke. Nest knew that the raiders wanted her husband dead, and she knew the castle. She told Gerald not to go out to the door, where they were waiting for him, and showed him a way out through the drain of the privy. While he slipped away into the dark, she went to the raiders and told them that he was gone.",
   "Owain carried Nest and her children away to Ceredigion. The news shook Wales and England alike. Henry I was furious, and the Norman lords and the Welsh princes who sided with them turned on Cadwgan and his sons. Owain fled to Ireland, and his father lost his lands for a time. The chronicle tells that Nest asked Owain to send her children home to their father, and he did. In time she herself returned to Gerald.",
   "The story of that night has been told ever since. Some called Nest the Helen of Wales, whose beauty set the princes of the south at war, as Helen’s had set the Greeks against Troy. But the heroine of Cenarth Bychan is a woman who kept her head in a burning castle and saved her husband’s life by her quick wits.",
   "## Her brother comes home",
   "In about 1113 Nest’s brother Gruffudd ap Rhys came back from Ireland, a grown man and the heir of Rhys ap Tewdwr. The chronicle says that he stayed for a while with his kinsmen and with Gerald, his sister’s husband. Then in 1116 he raised the young men of Deheubarth and attacked the Norman castles at Narberth, Llandovery and Swansea. He married Gwenllian, daughter of Gruffudd ap Cynan, and their son was the Lord Rhys, who would make Deheubarth great again.",
   "So the children of Rhys ap Tewdwr stood on both sides of the struggle for the south. Gruffudd fought the Normans for his father’s kingdom; Nest raised the sons who would carry the blood of the kings of Deheubarth into the Norman world. Both kept the line of Rhys alive.",
   "## The mother of the Geraldines",
   "Nest had other children too: a son, Robert, by Stephen, the constable of Cardigan, and others besides. Her sons grew up proud of their Welsh mother and spoke of themselves as men of both peoples. When Diarmait, the king of Leinster, came to Wales in 1167 looking for soldiers to win back his kingdom, it was to Nest’s sons that he turned.",
   "In May 1169 her son Robert FitzStephen landed at Bannow Bay in Wexford with archers and knights from Pembrokeshire and the south of Wales. Her son Maurice FitzGerald followed, and her grandson Meilyr FitzHenry fought beside them. It was the first landing of the men from Wales who began the Norman conquest of Ireland. Maurice’s sons founded the great Irish families of the FitzGeralds, the Earls of Kildare and of Desmond, who were among the most powerful in Ireland for four hundred years and are still called the Geraldines.",
   "Her son David became bishop of St Davids in 1148 and ruled the see of Dewi for almost thirty years. Her daughter Angharad married William de Barry of Manorbier, and their son was Gerald, born at Manorbier in about 1146.",
   "## Gerald of Wales, her grandson",
   "Gerald grew up hearing the stories of his grandmother’s family. He was proud of the royal blood of Rhys ap Tewdwr in his veins, and proud of his uncles who had won Ireland. He wrote the history of their deeds in his Conquest of Ireland. He fought for years to make St Davids an archbishopric again and Wales a church of its own, and he travelled round Wales in 1188 and wrote the Journey through Wales and the Description of Wales, the most vivid picture of the Welsh in the Middle Ages.",
   "In those books Gerald wrote with love of the land of his grandmother: its people, its music, its welcome to strangers, and its courage. When he set down the famous words of the old man of Pencader, that no nation but the Welsh would answer for this corner of the earth on the Day of Judgement, he was writing as the grandson of a princess of Deheubarth.",
   "## Why Nest matters",
   "Nest lived at the moment when the Norman conquest of Wales seemed complete, and she did not disappear. As the daughter of the last king of Deheubarth she kept the honour of her line; as the wife of the lord of Pembroke she held her place in her own country; as a mother she sent her sons into the history of Ireland and her grandson into the history of Wales.",
   "The kingdom of Rhys ap Tewdwr fell in 1093, but his blood ran on: in Gruffudd and the Lord Rhys, who won Deheubarth back; in the bishops of St Davids; in the Geraldines of Ireland; and in Gerald of Wales. Nest was the bridge between them all, and nine hundred years later she is still the most famous princess of the south."
